common --enable_bzlmod
test --test_output=errors --test_summary=terse
# Ζ·tier·toolchain — stamp every build with the toolchain fingerprint: STABLE_TOOLCHAIN_IMAGE, the
# digest of the executor image a `toolchain`-tier check runs in (tools/toolchain_status.sh →
# tools/image_digest.py → luthen's images_query, by name).  A toolchain-tier check (verb.bzl) is a
# STAMPED action depending on the stable status, so an image re-declare invalidates it precisely
# (measured: a changed stable key re-runs the action, an unchanged one is a cache hit) while an
# unchanged image keeps it cached.  The key is the fingerprint a consumer needs to trust which
# environment proved a claim (the VPAT tier disclosure).  ⚑ Until 2026-09-21 the keys were the
# CLIENT HOST's tool version banners — they described the box the command was typed on, not the
# pool the verdict ran in (Ζ·toolchain·declare).
# Ζ·jvm·raise — the COORDINATOR's heap, on the same climb ladder every sweep cell already uses.
#
# Bazel passes NO heap flag: src/main/cpp/startup_options.cc's AddJVMMemoryArguments is an empty
# stub (unnamed parameters, `return SUCCESS`), declared virtual and overridden nowhere — verified
# in the 8.7.0 dist and against the live server's argv, which carries only -Xverify:none and
# -XX:-MaxFDLimit.  So the ceiling is pure HotSpot ergonomics: MaxRAMPercentage=25 of MemTotal.
# Measured on this box: MemTotal/4 = 4095440896 against an observed MaxHeapSize of 4095737856
# — the ratio holds to 7.25e-05, which identifies the mechanism beyond doubt (MemTotal+Swap
# would give 14.10 GiB, MemAvailable 0.55).  NOT an exact match, and the heap is the LARGER
# by 296,960 bytes; no obvious granule reproduces it.  The earlier "exactly" was a
# two-decimal eyeball promoted to a precision nobody measured (peer's own correction).
#
# ⚑ THAT CEILING IS NOT ABSENT, IT IS TOO LOW — and it is the one number here nobody chose.
# Every sweep cell runs under a per-action cgroup lease; the server holding them all runs on a
# default derived from a machine it SHARES.  It died twice with `Build completed successfully,
# 32325 total actions` immediately above the OOM: all the work done, the JVM out of room to
# write up the result.
#
# ⚑⚑ AND SWAP IS NOT THE ANSWER, which is why this is a heap number and not a memory one.
# `OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space` is the collector refusing to grow past its own bound — the
# allocation never reaches the kernel, so there is no page for zram or zswap to receive.  The box
# had 27.9 GB of free swap and memory PSI peaked at ~4% across the crash window (cassian's host
# series, queried over 20:40-21:10).  Nothing failed to reclaim; nothing needed reclaiming.
#
# THE LADDER.  Same discipline as tools/cgroup-scope's OOM climb, one layer up: start at the
# rung above the observed failure and DOUBLE on the next one.  It OOM'd at 3.81 -> take 4G.  If
# it OOMs again -> 8G.  A too-small guess costs one retry and self-corrects; a too-large one
# holds RAM this box needs for the cells and for whatever else the operator is running.  Raising
# it far enough to break something else would, in the operator's phrasing, be an important lesson.
# ⚑ RUNG 2 (Ζ·jvm·8g), 2026-08-27.  4G OOM'd too, on a full 125,471-action run — so the ladder
# doubles, exactly as a cell's does.  And this time the climb is EVIDENCED rather than inferred:
# the coordinator sampler (Ρ·telemetry·red, landing in the same arc) caught the curve the old
# end-of-run push structurally could not — 2,479 samples over 41 minutes, RSS climbing 0.09G ->
# 4.34G, PEAKING AT 99% THROUGH THE RUN.  It dies writing up results, with every action already
# done, which is what `Build completed successfully` above the OOM had been saying all along.
# The instrument built for the failure caught the failure on its first occurrence, before it was
# even committed.
#
# ⚑ RUNG 3 (Ζ·jvm·12g), 2026-08-31.  8G OOM'd — same signature, `Build completed successfully,
# 26045 total actions` printed immediately above `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`,
# with every action and all 23 test targets done and the JVM dying while writing up the result.
#
# ⚑⚑ AND THE GRID GREW, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL REASON THE RUNG MOVED.  The 8G rung was measured
# against 125,471 actions; this run configured 152,367 — 21% more, from one arc: bibparse +
# bibfidelity + rematch entered the partition, paperkit/tools/ became a subpackage, and
# Ξ·dag·script widened edge-formulas's closure to bib + bibparse (its grid re-ran, 26,013 fresh
# cells against 126,347 cache hits).  A heap sized for the old graph does not fit the new one.
# So this is not "8G was wrong"; it is that the ceiling tracks GRAPH SIZE and nobody re-derived
# it when the graph moved.  The next arc that adds modules moves it again.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ 16G ON A 15.6 GB BOX, AND THAT IS NOT THE CONTRADICTION IT LOOKS LIKE.  A first cut of
# this rung stopped at 12G reasoning that -Xmx above physical RAM removes the collector's
# back-pressure and lets the server "grow into swap and drag the machine".  That argument was
# built on a wrong model of THIS machine (operator's correction: *this machine leverages zram and
# zswap; it's not what you assume*), and it also mis-cited the note above.
#
#   * MEASURED: zram0 is a 9.2G zstd-compressed RAM block device holding 7.6G of data in 2G —
#     a 3.9x ratio — with zswap (lz4, max_pool_percent=20) in front of it.  Paging here is a
#     compressor, not a platter; the cost is CPU on cold pages, not seek latency.  So `free -m`
#     reporting 928 MB free is not the number it looks like either.
#   * AND THE NOTE ABOVE MAKES A NARROWER CLAIM THAN I BORROWED IT FOR.  "Swap is not the answer"
#     is true because `OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space` is the collector refusing to grow past
#     -Xmx: the allocation never reaches the kernel, so NO swap tier — compressed or otherwise —
#     is ever consulted.  That says swap cannot SUBSTITUTE for raising the ceiling.  It does not
#     say a raised ceiling is dangerous, which is the step I added.
#
# So the ladder's own discipline applies unmodified: it OOM'd at 8G, so take 16G.  The JVM maps
# what it touches rather than reserving the ceiling, and the pages it does touch and then abandon
# are exactly the compressible kind.  If 16G OOMs, that is the measurement saying this graph no
# longer fits this box, and the answer becomes splitting //:hook — not another gigabyte.
startup --host_jvm_args=-Xmx16g
# ⚑ Ζ·jvm·parallel — PARALLEL, NOT G1, FOR A ONE-SHOT BATCH WORKLOAD (operator, 2026-09-08).
# G1 is a LOW-PAUSE collector: it spends CPU and memory overhead (region metadata, remembered
# sets, SATB write barriers, concurrent marking threads) to keep individual pauses short.  A
# build does not care about pause time — nobody minds a 4-second stop, they mind whether the
# build finishes — so that overhead is paid and never repaid.
#
# MEASURED on the live server (pid 1706204, jstat via hsperfdata, four reads minutes apart):
#   OC = 16,777,216.0 KB (the full -Xmx16g)   OU = 16,769,987.9 KB   -> 99.96% FULL
#   FGC 28 -> 32 across the reads, FGCT 4923 -> 5279 s, and OU DID NOT MOVE
#   CGC = 11, CGCT = 20.449 s
#   LGCC = G1 Evacuation Pause, GCC = G1 Compaction Pause  (G1 in last-resort full compaction)
#
# ⚑⚑ THE OPERATOR'S FIRST FRAMING WAS "disable incremental compaction", and the numbers REFINE
# it rather than confirm it: the incremental side is CGCT=20.4s of 5575s total GC — 0.4%.  There
# is no incremental cost to remove; the spiral is ALREADY 100% full-GC.  So the lever is not
# disabling a phase of G1, it is not running a concurrent collector at all.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND THIS IS NOT THE FIX FOR THE UNDERLYING PROBLEM, STATED PLAINLY SO THE NEXT READER
# DOES NOT MISTAKE IT FOR ONE.  32 full GCs freeing ZERO bytes means the LIVE SET does not fit —
# that is data, not garbage, and no collector reclaims live data.  ParallelGC gets more USABLE
# heap from the same -Xmx (no region metadata, no remembered sets, simpler layout), which buys
# headroom; it does not make a too-large graph fit.  The rc's own escape clause above still
# stands and is now overdue: "If 16G OOMs, that is the measurement saying this graph no longer
# fits this box, and the answer becomes splitting //:hook — not another gigabyte."
#
# VERIFIED ACCEPTED on this box's JDK 25 before landing: -XX:+PrintFlagsFinal reports
# `UseParallelGC = true {command line}` and `UseG1GC = false {default}`.
startup --host_jvm_args=-XX:+UseParallelGC

build --stamp --workspace_status_command=tools/toolchain_status.sh
# Ζ·toolchain·declare — `build --action_env=HOME` WAS here and is REMOVED, 2026-09-21.  It carried
# the client's $HOME into toolchain-tier actions because part of the toolchain lived there
# (~/texmf's luaotfload.sty et al., ~/.local caches) — 169 files the build graph never declared
# (operator: hashing them "indicates the build graph isn't properly built out").  The toolchain
# now lives in the executor image (image/executor/Containerfile), where TeX Live's own tree is
# the TEXMF root; nothing an action needs is under anyone's home, so nothing forwards it.  If a
# check still reaches for $HOME the pool will NAME it, which is the point.
# Memory bounding is Τ·mem: each pk_calc declares a resource_set (the learned mem.json bucket via
# the bib generator's ladder), and Bazel's local scheduler bounds concurrent sweeps against
# --local_ram_resources (default HOST_RAM*.67) — no rc pool needed.  The old --local_extra_resources
# mem_mb pool (the membudget→tags projection) was never wired and is retired.
# Τ·mem·observe — isolate each action in its own cgroup so it can read its OWN tree-peak memory
# (/sys/fs/cgroup/<action>/memory.peak — the only tree-accurate channel; Bazel's log has no peak,
# and systemd-run is dead in the scrubbed sandbox env).  OPT-IN (needs cgroup-v2 + the new cgroup
# impl), so default builds stay portable on non-cgroup machines; run `bazel build … --config=memobserve`
# to populate the learned-mem manifest, which then refines the portable reservations for everyone.
build:memobserve --incompatible_use_new_cgroup_implementation --experimental_sandbox_memory_limit_mb=HOST_RAM
# Τ·mem·observe·clean — turn the peak read ON only here; default builds write a clean 0 (the read OFF
# would be the SHARED-cgroup garbage), and the flag is part of the calc action key (observe-vs-default
# cache as distinct actions, so no stale measured/0 peak crosses configs).
build:memobserve --//tools:observe=True

# Ζ·mutant·eval — the def-sweep's counterfactuals run as NORMAL actions in the HERMETIC linux-sandbox
# (hardlinks + chroot), so a check's Path(__file__).resolve() and its subprocesses CANNOT follow
# symlinks out to the source tree — they see only the staged inputs (the mutated engine at its
# canonical path).  The system dirs are mount-paired for bash/python+libs; the SOURCE tree is NOT
# mounted, so it stays unreachable.  Standard-FHS dirs → portable across Linux.
#
# ⚑ Ζ·mutant·default — THESE ARE `build`, NOT `build:mutant`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
# They were opt-in, and an opt-in hermeticity flag is not hermeticity: a bare `bazel test` fell back
# to the ORDINARY linux-sandbox, whose execroot is a directory of SYMLINKS INTO THE SOURCE TREE, so
# a check's Path(__file__).resolve() walked straight back out to /home/…/paperkit and read HOST
# sources that the action never declared.  That is a false GREEN available to anyone who forgets a
# flag — the one outcome this engine exists to refuse — and it is not hypothetical:
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-02, @paperkit_boundaries//:gate, both arms forced (--nouse_action_cache) so
# neither could serve the other's cached verdict:
#   with the flags:     7 RED, every traceback rooted at /execroot/_main/… — no host prefix
#   without the flags: 10 RED, tracebacks rooted at …/sandbox/linux-sandbox/N/execroot/…, and the
#                      three extra reds are ESCAPES, not check failures:
#     bnd-closure-script  ImportError: cannot import name 'closure_census' from 'tools'
#                         (/home/mikemol/github/paperkit/paperkit/tools/__init__.py)  ← HOST tree
#     bnd-scope           OSError: [Errno 30] Read-only file system:
#                         '/home/mikemol/github/paperkit/paperkit/tests/_scope_tmp.bib'  ← HOST path
#     bnd-hook-index      ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tools.indexdiverge'
#   The bare arm resolved the string `tools` against the HOST package and got a DIFFERENT module set
#   than the staged inputs define.  Which arm is red is not the finding; WHICH TREE THEY READ is.
#
# ⚑ AND THE SANDBOX FLAGS ARE NOT IN THE ACTION KEY, which is why this could not stay a convention.
# Measured: `bazel build @paperkit_boundaries//:bnd-cache` with and then without --config=mutant is a
# CACHE HIT both ways.  So a verdict computed under one regime is served under the other, and the
# difference is invisible after the first run — [[bazel-action-idempotency]] again, with the
# EXECUTION REGIME as the undeclared input.  A flag that changes what an action can read but not
# what it is keyed on cannot be left to the caller to remember.
#
# ⚑ THE local/toolchain TIERS ARE UNAFFECTED, and that was the thing to check before promoting.
# verb.bzl's _tier_exec puts "no-sandbox": "1" in execution_requirements for both, which selects the
# `local` spawn strategy at the ACTION level — before any sandbox flag is consulted.  MEASURED:
# `bazel build @paperkit_setup//:swap-zswap @paperkit_setup//:mach-cores --config=mutant` reports
# `3 processes: 1 internal, 2 local` — the host-coupled probes still run on the host, unsandboxed,
# reading the live /proc and /sys they exist to read.  Promotion does not touch them.
# --config=mutant-oci overrides per-mnemonic (--strategy=PkEval=docker), so it still wins here too.
build --experimental_use_hermetic_linux_sandbox
build --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/bin --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/usr
build --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/lib --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/lib64 --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/etc
# `--config=mutant` is KEPT AS A NO-OP so every existing invocation stays valid: .githooks/pre-commit
# passes it, so do the docs and the downstream consumers, and an rc with no `build:mutant` line at
# all makes `--config=mutant` a hard ERROR.  Retiring the name is a separate, wider change; making
# the behaviour unconditional is this one.  δ = the three lines above changed prefix, nothing else.
build:mutant --experimental_use_hermetic_linux_sandbox

# Ζ·mutant·oci — the FULLY hermetic route: each counterfactual runs in a CONTAINER from the
# proof-object Κ·image (paperkit-base:proof carries python+bash+strace), no host /usr at all —
# the proof image IS the verification environment (Κ·image ↔ Ζ·mutant).  use_customized_images=false
# runs the action as the image root → rootless podman maps it to the host uid, so bazel-out is
# writable (no Bazel uid-add, no keep-id).  Prereq: build the image once —
#   podman build --timestamp 0 -t paperkit-base:proof -f Containerfile.base .
build:mutant-oci --experimental_enable_docker_sandbox
build:mutant-oci --experimental_docker_image=localhost/paperkit-base:proof
build:mutant-oci --experimental_docker_use_customized_images=false
# ⚑ Ζ·oci·spawn — THE DEFAULT, NOT TWO NAMED MNEMONICS.  This line read
#   --strategy=PkEval=docker --strategy=PkMutate=docker
# and the engine defines 28 action mnemonics across tools/{calc,verb,grade,witness}.bzl.  Two were
# containerised; twenty-six silently ran on the host under a config whose whole claim is "no host
# /usr at all" — and a 29th mnemonic added tomorrow would opt out with no signal at all.  That is a
# hermeticity config that is hermetic for 7% of the graph, which is the false-GREEN shape this rc
# already refuses one section up (Ζ·mutant·default: an opt-in hermeticity flag is not hermeticity).
#
# MEASURED against the live binary (bazel 8.7.0, `bazel help build --long`): --strategy's mnemonic is
# OPTIONAL — its grammar is literally `[name=]value1[,..,valueN]` — and --spawn_strategy is the
# purpose-built flag, documented as "Specify how spawn actions are executed by default".  So the
# DEFAULT is settable, and setting it DISSOLVES the enumeration rather than maintaining it.
#
# ⚑ A DRIFT GUARD WAS CONSIDERED AND DECLINED, and the reasoning is the point.  The additive fix is
# a check asserting "every `mnemonic =` in the .bzl files appears in this strategy list".  That is
# [[guard-must-not-copy]]: the enumeration does not disappear, it MOVES from .bazelrc into the
# checker, which then carries its own copy of the set it guards and needs its own maintenance on the
# 29th mnemonic.  A guard holding a copy of the set certifies a tautology.  Setting the default
# removes the set from the rc entirely, so there is nothing left to drift — the reductive fix.
#
# ⚑ THE FALLBACK IS LOAD-BEARING, priority-ordered highest-to-lowest: anything docker cannot execute
# falls through to sandboxed, then local, rather than failing the build.  In particular verb.bzl's
# _tier_exec puts {"local": "1", "no-sandbox": "1"} in execution_requirements for the local and
# toolchain tiers, which selects the `local` strategy AT THE ACTION LEVEL before any strategy list is
# consulted — setup's host-coupled /proc and /sys probes are not containerised by this line and could
# not be.  Keeping `local` in the list is belt-and-braces on a property the action requirements
# already guarantee (the same check the Ζ·mutant·default promotion note above records making).
#
# ⚑ UNVERIFIED, AND NOT RESOLVED BY GUESSING: whether all 28 mnemonics can actually EXECUTE under
# localhost/paperkit-base:proof.  It could not be tested here — MEASURED 2026-09-02, `podman images`
# on this box has no paperkit-base:proof at all, so --config=mutant-oci is unrunnable until the
# prereq build above is run.  The fallback means an image-incapable mnemonic degrades to sandboxed
# rather than erroring, which makes a gap quiet; the first real mutant-oci run should read the
# `N processes: … docker` line to confirm the split.
build:mutant-oci --spawn_strategy=docker,sandboxed,local

# ═══ Ζ·cas — THE REMOTE ACTION CACHE, AND WHY IT REPLACES A PLANNED CONTAINER ═══════════════
#
# ⚑ THE DEFECT IT FIXES: THE EXECROOT PATH IS A HIDDEN INPUT TO EVERY ACTION KEY.
# Bazel derives its output base from an md5 of the WORKSPACE PATH, so the same tree at two paths
# is two caches that share nothing.  MEASURED 2026-08-29, before the wipe: seven output bases
# under ~/.cache/bazel/_bazel_mikemol, of which two were 1.8G and 1.9G for THIS repo at two paths
# — 3.7 GB of near-duplicate cache — and a worktree build ran 129,577 actions against 4,631 cache
# hits while an identical tree sat fully built beside it.  That is [[bazel-action-idempotency]]
# violated at the harness level: an action must be f(inputs) alone, and here the PATH was an input.
#
# ⚑ WHY A CACHE AND NOT A CONTAINER.  The plan was to bind-mount every repo to a fixed path
# (/work) so the execroot would be constant.  That works, and it is the WEAKER construction: it
# makes paths agree BY CONVENTION, so every future worktree, repo and machine must adopt the same
# mount to benefit, and a single divergent invocation silently re-forks the cache.  A remote action
# cache is content-addressed and path-independent BY PROTOCOL — the CAS has no concept of a
# worktree — so the property holds without anyone remembering to mount anything.  The container was
# the intermediate rung; this is the terminus.  (The mutant-oci sandbox above stays, for a DIFFERENT
# property: hermeticity, no host /usr.  Containerising the ACTION was never the same as
# containerising the PATH, and conflating them is what made the container look sufficient.)
#
# THE ENDPOINT.  buildbuddy-app-onprem, already running on this box as the podman container
# ⚑ ENDPOINTS MOVED 2026-09-04 (cassian's ◆gate-k8s-execution): the podman quadlet BuildBuddy is
# DISABLED and the control plane now runs IN k3s.  gRPC 1985 -> 31985 (loopback-pinned NodePort),
# web 8080 -> 31080.  VERIFIED HERE before changing anything: :1985 closed, :31985 open.  The
# sentence below said it was NOT in the k3s cluster; that is now false, and it is kept struck
# rather than deleted because the reason it was true (a podman container outside the cluster) is
# what the migration changed.  It WAS in `cassian-buildbuddy` (gRPC 1985, web 8080), NOT in the
# k3s cluster that manages
# everything else here — it is the same bespoke shape as the builds it serves, and moving it in is
# its own arc.  Pointing Bazel at it WHERE IT IS first is deliberate: if remote caching misbehaves,
# the diagnosis is the caching and not a concurrent migration.
#
# ⚑ THIS WAS OPT-IN ON A FALSE-GREEN ARGUMENT, AND THE ARGUMENT WAS WRONG AT ITS ROOT.  It read:
# "an unproven cache that silently serves a wrong artifact is strictly worse than no cache — it
# converts a build failure into a false GREEN", gated behind two promotion conditions (hits across
# two worktree paths; byte-identical output vs a cold local build).  Superseded 2026-09-07:
#
#   there is no false-green risk if the cache keys are constructed properly, and if they are NOT
#   constructed properly, the false-green risk does not depend on peers to cause the collision
#
# A wrong key collides with THIS repo's own prior invocation on one machine exactly as readily as
# with a peer's.  So sharing changes the BLAST RADIUS and not the soundness, and gating adoption on
# sharing was guarding the wrong variable.  The real precondition is correct keys — the open
# Ζ·closure·owner arc (a cell staging 23 flat engine sources where calc.bzl's PycInfo already
# carries a transitive cone; 8.4% hit rate on a one-line edit) — and that is owed whether or not
# anything else ever reads this cache.  MEASURED here, three arms with `bazel clean` between each:
# --remote_instance_name=DELIBERATELY_WRONG missed and re-executed, the correct name returned
# "1 remote cache hit" — the CONTROL, without which a miss is indistinguishable from a dead CAS.
#
# --remote_upload_local_results: WRITE as well as read.  A read-only cache on a single machine is
# a no-op — nothing else populates it.
# ⚑ Ζ·cas·adopt — --remote_local_fallback WAS set here and is now REMOVED, deliberately.  The
# rationale it carried ("a cache that is DOWN must not fail the build ... degrades to a local
# build rather than reporting a red gate for an infrastructure reason") is REFUTED by what the
# fallback degrades TO: an uncoordinated local build is the resource hazard this configuration
# exists to prevent, so the flag is not a safety measure but an escape hatch FROM the policy,
# firing silently on every affected machine at the moment shared infrastructure is under stress.
# A red for an unreachable CAS is now the INTENDED outcome — a loud failure that stops work,
# not a quiet degradation that consumes the box.
# --remote_instance_name: the CAS is SHARED across repos with different toolchains and action
# keys.  An unnamed instance is one namespace for all of them; the name is the partition.
# --remote_cache_async: do not block the critical path on uploads.
build:cas --remote_cache=grpc://buildbuddy-bes.buildbuddy.svc.cluster.local:1985
build:cas --remote_upload_local_results=true
build:cas --remote_instance_name=paperkit
build:cas --remote_cache_async=true          # (was --experimental_remote_cache_async, deprecated in 8.7)
# Name the run in buildbuddy's UI, so a CAS question can be answered from the artifact rather than
# from a guess about which invocation wrote what.
build:cas --bes_results_url=http://buildbuddy-web.buildbuddy.svc.cluster.local:8080/invocation/
build:cas --bes_backend=grpc://buildbuddy-bes.buildbuddy.svc.cluster.local:1985

# ⚑⚑ ARMED — `cas` IS PROMOTED TO THE DEFAULT, which is what makes every line above load-bearing.
# MEASURED 2026-09-07, before this block existed: `bazel build --nobuild //paperkit:__init__` with
# no `--config` printed NO `Streaming build results to:` line, and a fleet reading put paperkit
# alone among four bazel trees with every endpoint behind an unpromoted config — so a bare
# `bazel test`, and the `--config=mutant` the pre-commit hook invokes, drew NOTHING from the shared
# CAS, contributed NOTHING to it, and emitted no BES stream.  A config nobody passes is a config
# nobody uses: the block was correct and inert, which reads identically to absent.
# Promotion by CONFIG rather than by flag (linux-sources' shape) keeps the rationale above attached
# to the flags it explains, instead of scattering four endpoint flags onto bare `build` lines.
build --config=cas
# (`test` inherits `build`, so a `test --config=cas` line here is the SECOND expansion bazel
# warned about — `[cas] expanded more than once`, measured 2026-09-21 with it present.)

# ⚑ Ζ·keepgoing·closure — STOP AT THE FIRST FAILURE.  Under a correct closure a failed cell forces
# a fix, the fix invalidates that cell AND everything downstream of the inputs it touched, and
# computing those first is waste: they are neither CONTEXT for the diagnosis nor a TRIGGER for a
# further fix.  What the fix does NOT invalidate is already in the action cache, so stopping early
# costs nothing — which is what makes fail-fast free rather than merely impatient, and why it is
# the CLOSURES that earn the flag rather than the flag that earns itself.
#
# MEASURED on the run that motivated this: `boundaries//:gate` reported FAIL at log line 971 and
# the invocation continued to 19,520 actions executed — ~18,500 of them computed after a known
# failure whose fix touches `boundaries/warrants.bib`, a file the whole boundaries grid keys on.
# A second failure (Ζ·eval concept-views__config__flip_positionals_arm_2, "not all outputs were
# created or valid") sat at line 2,773 and went unread for four ticks because the tail was
# advancing and nothing surfaced it.
#
# ⚑⚑ TWO FLAGS, OPPOSITE DEFAULTS, AND THE FIRST DRAFT SET THE WRONG ONE.  This block once said
# `--nokeep_going` was what stopped the run at a red gate.  It is not, and it was already the
# default — setting it changed nothing.  From bazel 8.7.0's own source (linux-sources read it at
# a6d8d66737d5; this tree runs 8.7.0, verified, so the citations apply without a version gap):
#
#   KeepGoingOption.java   name = "keep_going",      defaultValue = "false"
#   ExecutionOptions.java  name = "test_keep_going", defaultValue = "true"
#     "When disabled, any non-passing test will cause the entire build to stop.  By default all
#      tests are run, even if some do not pass."
#
# THREE FAILURE SHAPES, THREE MECHANISMS, and only one is a test:
#   (a) a pk_* CELL exits 0 and emits a verdict record — NOT a failure to bazel at any layer.
#       Correct by design: bazel sees a red ACTION, never a red CHECK.
#   (b) `FAIL: //:gate (Exit 1)` — a TEST failure, governed by test_keep_going.
#   (c) `not all outputs were created or valid` — an ACTION failure (SkyframeActionExecutor's
#       ACTION_OUTPUTS_NOT_CREATED), governed by keep_going.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE RUN THAT MOTIVATED THIS HIT (b) AT LINE 971 AND (c) AT LINE 2,773 — two failures needing
# two different flags, and the one set governed the SECOND.  The 18,500 wasted actions are the gap
# between them.  Both flags land on one field (AggregatingTestListener's `skipTargetsOnFailure`,
# "in the presence of --nokeep_going or --notest_keep_going"), which is why the wrong one LOOKED
# like it should have worked.  The doc warns against `--notest_keep_going` with `--keep_going` —
# the CONTRADICTORY pair; the pair below is the consistent one.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ THE COST IS REAL AND ACCEPTED: fail-fast is free only when the failure is OBSERVABLE IN
# FLIGHT.  --test_summary=terse withholds verdicts until the end, so the stopping signal arrives
# through the BES stream and the log rather than a summary — which is why the CAS/BES arming above
# is a precondition for this line rather than an unrelated setting.
build --nokeep_going
test --nokeep_going
test --notest_keep_going

# ═══ Ζ·rbe — REMOTE EXECUTION, NOT JUST THE CACHE ══════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The same gRPC endpoint (:31985 since the k3s move) serves a SCHEDULER as well as BES + cache:
# cassian's buildbuddy gained a
# remote executor, and this box is the same box, so loopback reaches it.
#
# ⚑ WHY, AND IT IS NOT SPEED.  The executor runs each action in a WRAPPER IMAGE whose rootfs is
# COPIED, so an action's in-place writes are contained and cannot escape into the source tree —
# unlike Bazel's local sandbox, which symlinks first-party packages straight back to the working
# tree (measured this session: $EXECROOT/paperkit is a symlink to the live checkout, which is how
# a check could read UNSTAGED sources and pass).  A failing action leaves its debris in a copy
# that is thrown away.  That is a containment property, and it is the reason to switch.
#
# ⚑⚑ THE CONSTRAINT THIS ENGINE OWNS IS HONORED.  Scheduling is governed by the learned per-action
# mem.json ladder (the resource_set the bib generator projects); the executor gives a generous
# budget and does NOT admission-control over those client hints.  Confirmed by the operator of the
# executor, unprompted, rather than assumed here.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ TWO BOUNDS, STATED BECAUSE THEY DECIDE WHERE THIS IS USED.  (1) It is a SINGLE executor —
# one host, one worker, no shared pool — so a cold ~137k-action //:hook would SERIALIZE through it
# and be slower than local.  Use it for the sweeps and the gc-sensitive work, measure before
# leaning on it for the whole hook.  (2) require_executor_authorization is OFF: unauthenticated on
# loopback, single-operator trust.  Fine on one box; not a property to inherit anywhere else.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ WHY THE EXECUTOR EXISTS — AND IT IS NOT SPEED.  Operator, 2026-09-21: "The remote executors
# were _created_ because of integrity problems with paperkit's local sandbox runs."  The two
# bounds above weigh the executor against local as if local were the baseline and remote the
# optimisation.  That is backwards.  Local sandbox runs are what the executor was built to REPLACE;
# a //:hook that "serializes through it and is slower than local" is slower than something that
# was not producing trustworthy verdicts in the first place.  Every September 2026 gate run on the
# previous host reported `remote-cache, linux-sandbox` — cache consulted, execution LOCAL — and
# under this framing none of them was a run.  The 118-path cut those runs "gated" has never been
# gated with integrity by construction; its first such run is on luthen.
#
# ⚑ Ζ·remote·nofallback — LOCAL FALLBACK IS REFUSED, BY DECLARATION.  Operator, same exchange:
# "hard 'no' on the sandbox fallback. the remote executor is what grants you integrity by
# construction."  This block used to argue the opposite — "an executor that is DOWN must not fail
# the build ... it is an instrument, not a gate" — and its own reason is the refutation: a
# fallback "has not yet been shown to produce byte-identical outputs to a cold local build", which
# is precisely why a local result may never stand in for a remote one.  A cell that ran in
# linux-sandbox under --config=remote produced a verdict WITHOUT the property that makes verdicts
# trustworthy; the run is not green-with-a-note, it is not a run.
#
# Bazel's default for --remote_local_fallback is already false, but ABSENT is weaker than REFUSED:
# a later command-line flag or a stray `build:` line would re-enable it silently.  Pinned off here
# so the declaration is the policy.  An executor that is down reds the build with a typed
# infrastructure exit (34/36/37/38/39 — see Ζ·exit·infra), which is the honest outcome.
build:remote --noremote_local_fallback
# (no `--config=cas` here: `build --config=cas` above already applies it to every command, and a
# second expansion is what `[cas] expanded more than once` warned about — measured 2026-09-21.)
# ⚑ The addresses below and in `cas` are PROJECTED by tools/project_endpoints.py from the host's
# endpoint directory (luthen-observability, by service name through the dns-delegate) — never
# edit them by hand; `python3 tools/project_endpoints.py --check` reports drift.
build:remote --remote_executor=grpc://buildbuddy-bes.buildbuddy.svc.cluster.local:1985
build:remote --remote_instance_name=paperkit
